## 4. Semantic risks (SEM-*)

Scanned against `02_rules/05_semantic_rules.md` (SEM-01…SEM-10). The statement is a 33-column `INSERT … SELECT … WHERE NOT EXISTS` anti-join: staging rows are inserted into `BASE.FLT_FOLDER_LINKS` only when no existing target row matches on all 33 columns. Semantic risks live in the comparison predicates of that anti-join and in the staging→target type binding.

| ID | Risk | Present? | Where (lines) | Classification | Detail |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| SEM-01 | Integer division truncation | No | — | — | No `/` operator in the statement. |
| SEM-02 | `QUALIFY` / dedup tie ordering | No | — | — | No `QUALIFY` / `ROW_NUMBER`. |
| **SEM-03** | **`CHAR(n)` padding & comparison** | **Yes** | anti-join predicates, lines 74–100 | **SME** | The 33-key `NOT EXISTS` compares `BASE.FLT_FOLDER_LINKS` against `STAGING.ExpFinalyseInserts` on all 33 columns. Several keys are plausibly `CHAR(n)` by name convention (`*_CD`, `*_TYP`, `*_TXT`, `*_IND`, `*_DUR`). Teradata blank-pads `CHAR(n)` and ignores trailing spaces in `=`; Snowflake does **not** pad, so `'A' <> 'A '`. A padding difference on any CHAR key silently changes which rows the anti-join suppresses — either inserting duplicates (a match missed) or suppressing valid rows (a false match). The mechanical validator cannot detect this without DDL for both sides. **Requires target + staging DDL to confirm which columns are `CHAR(n)`; if any are, wrap the corresponding predicates in `RTRIM(T.col) = RTRIM(S.col)` (or cast both sides to `VARCHAR`) to preserve Teradata semantics.** Flag `TODO(SME)`. |
| SEM-04 | Timestamp / timezone | Possible | lines 42–44, 53–55 (`*_FLTDT`, `*_FLTTM` vs `*_DATE` aliases) | SME (subsumed under SEM-06) | The `*_FLTDT` / `*_FLTTM` target columns are compared against `NEW_*_DATE` staging aliases. If either side is `TIMESTAMP`/`TIMESTAMP_TZ` and the other is `DATE`/`TIMESTAMP_NTZ`, the comparison grain shifts. Without DDL this cannot be confirmed; folded into the SEM-06 type-compatibility finding below. |
| **SEM-05** | **SET-table dedup** | **No (not triggered)** | — | — | Single `INSERT` into one target, no `UNION`/`UNION ALL` branches writing to the same target. The SET-vs-MULTISET question does **not** arise from this SQL alone. (The `NOT EXISTS` anti-join is an explicit application-level dedup, not a SET-table dedup.) No SME question raised — per the mode rule, SEM-05 is only raised when multiple INSERTs or a multi-branch UNION targets one table and no DDL is supplied. |
| **SEM-06** | **Implicit cast / NULL in comparison** | **Yes** | anti-join predicates, lines 74–100; SELECT→INSERT binding, lines 40–65 | **SME** | Two facets: (a) The `_SMALLINT` / `_DATE` suffix convention on staging aliases (`NEW_IN_FLT_NO_SMALLINT`, `NEW_*_FLTDT_DATE`, `NEW_*_FLTTM_DATE`, `NEW_MIDNIGHT_QTY_SMALLINT`, etc.) signals pre-cast staging values, but the `NOT EXISTS` predicates compare these against `BASE.FLT_FOLDER_LINKS` columns whose types are unknown. A type mismatch (e.g. `DATE` vs `TIMESTAMP`, `VARCHAR` vs `NUMBER`, `SMALLINT` vs `NUMBER(38)`) could silently change which rows the anti-join suppresses via implicit cast. The suffix describes the *staging* cast, not the *target* column — do not assume it is authoritative. (b) `NULL` handling: the outer `WHERE` guards `IN_FLT_LEG_SEQ_NO IS NOT NULL AND OUT_FLT_LEG_SEQ_NO IS NOT NULL`, but the other 31 keys are not NULL-guarded; a NULL on any other key makes the `=` predicate NULL (false), which is correct for anti-join semantics, but if the Teradata original relied on `NULLIF`/`COALESCE` on specific keys, that intent is not visible here. **Requires DDL for both `BASE.FLT_FOLDER_LINKS` and `STAGING.ExpFinalyseInserts` to confirm type compatibility on every compared column.** Flag `TODO(SME)` on each `_SMALLINT`/`_DATE`-suffixed alias that flows into a predicate. |
| SEM-07 | `NULL` ordering in `ORDER BY` feeding `QUALIFY`/`TOP` | No | — | — | No `ORDER BY` / `QUALIFY` / `TOP`. |
| SEM-08 | Empty string vs NULL | Possible | `*_RSN_TXT`, `*_IND` keys | SME (subsumed under SEM-03/06) | If `FIXED_LINK_RSN_TXT` or `CURRENT_REC_IND` can be `''` vs NULL, the anti-join `=` treats them differently on Snowflake than Teradata may have. Cannot confirm without DDL/data contract; folded into the SEM-03/06 SME question. |
| SEM-09 | Aggregate of empty set / `SUM` NULL | No | — | — | No aggregates. |
