# Stage 6 — Semantic explanation: `SFIssueSET`

**Fixed file:** `03_fix/output/SFIssueSET_fixed.sql`
**Teradata ground truth:** NOT supplied (no `00_input/SFIssueSET.teradata.sql`).
Every semantic inference below is flagged `TODO(SME)` and must be confirmed by
a subject-matter expert before sign-off.
**Reviewer-confirmed fact:** `staging.wrk_target` is a **SET table** (see
`02_rules/06_lessons_learned.md`, 2026-07-25 entry). This closes the SEM-05
question and is the basis of the structural fix.

---

## 1. Purpose

This script populates the SET staging table `staging.wrk_target` with the
"latest" operating attributes (company code, direction indicator, operating
airline / flight number / suffix) for each marketing flight leg, taken from
three different leg sources — the **marketing** leg, the **operational** leg,
and the **codeshare** leg. The grain of one output row is one flight
`(airline_cd, flgt_no, flgt_dt)`. For each source leg, the script joins the
flight source `synthetic_flgt_source` to the leg table on the airline/flight/
date keys, filters to legs where the departure station differs from the
arrival station and the flight date is after the `UPDATE_CUTOFF` system
parameter, then collapses the joined rows per flight using the
`SUBSTR(MAX(effective_dt || val), 11, N)` "latest effective value" trick. Because
the target is a SET table, each branch is written as an
`INSERT … (SELECT … EXCEPT SELECT * FROM staging.wrk_target)` so full-row
duplicates already present are silently dropped — preserving Teradata SET
semantics.

---

## 2. Clause-by-clause walk-through

### Branch 1 — marketing leg (`synthetic_mkg_flgt_leg`)

- **JOIN:** `synthetic_flgt_source src` is joined to `synthetic_mkg_flgt_leg leg`
  on `src.airline_cd = leg.marketing_airline_cd`, `src.flgt_no =
  leg.marketing_flgt_no`, `src.flgt_dt = leg.gmt_flgt_dt`. This attaches the
  marketing-leg operating attributes to each source flight.
- **WHERE:** `leg.dep_stn_cd <> leg.arr_stn_cd` excludes trivial same-station
  legs; `src.flgt_dt > (SELECT param_value FROM synthetic_sys_param WHERE
  param_id = 'UPDATE_CUTOFF')` restricts to flights after the configured
  cutoff date.
- **GROUP BY / MAX trick:** Rows are grouped by `(airline_cd, flgt_no,
  flgt_dt)`. For each group, `MAX(src.effective_dt || leg.<attr>)` concatenates
  the effective date (as a sortable string) with the attribute value, takes the
  maximum (i.e. the row with the latest effective date), then `SUBSTR(..., 11,
  N)` strips the date prefix to recover the attribute. This is the classic
  Teradata "latest effective value" pattern.
- **EXCEPT dedup:** `EXCEPT SELECT * FROM staging.wrk_target` removes any row
  that already exists in the target, so the SET table never receives a
  full-row duplicate. The buggy input instead used a single
  `INSERT … UNION … UNION`, which deduplicated *across* branches (wrong axis)
  and re-inserted rows already present — violating SET semantics.

### Branch 2 — operational leg (`synthetic_operational_flgt_leg`)

Identical structure to branch 1, but joins on the **operating** keys
(`src.airline_cd = leg.operating_airline_cd`, `src.flgt_no =
leg.operating_flgt_no`, `src.flgt_dt = leg.gmt_flgt_dt`) and sources
`synthetic_operational_flgt_leg`. Same `dep_stn_cd <> arr_stn_cd` filter, same
cutoff, same MAX trick, same `EXCEPT SELECT * FROM staging.wrk_target` dedup.

### Branch 3 — codeshare leg (`synthetic_codeshare_flgt_leg`)

Identical structure, joining on the **codeshare** keys
(`src.airline_cd = cs.codeshare_airline_cd`, `src.flgt_no =
cs.codeshare_flgt_no`, `src.flgt_dt = cs.gmt_flgt_dt`) and sourcing
`synthetic_codeshare_flgt_leg` (alias `cs`). Same filters, MAX trick, and
EXCEPT dedup.

### What the buggy input would have done instead

The buggy input combined all three branches with `UNION` inside one
`INSERT INTO staging.wrk_target` with no explicit column list. `UNION`
deduplicates *across* the three branches (so a row produced by two branches
would be inserted once, not twice), and it re-inserts any row already present
in the target. For a SET table this is wrong: SET dedup is per-row against the
existing target contents, not across branches. The fix splits the single
INSERT into three independent `INSERT … (SELECT … EXCEPT SELECT * FROM
staging.wrk_target)` statements and adds an explicit 8-column INSERT target
list so the `EXCEPT SELECT *` positional match is unambiguous.

---

## 3. Semantic checks table

| SEM ID | Risk | Present? | Corrected how? | Equivalent to Teradata? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| SEM-01 | Integer division | No | n/a — no `/` in the script | n/a |
| SEM-02 | `QUALIFY`/dedup tie ordering | Yes (FN-LATEST trick) | Not corrected — `MAX(effective_dt \|\| val)` kept as-is pending SME confirmation of `effective_dt` type and value widths | SME — if `effective_dt` is DATE the implicit `\|\|` cast is session-format-dependent and `MAX` can pick the wrong row |
| SEM-03 | `CHAR(n)` padding & comparison | Possibly (join keys) | Not corrected — pending DDL; if any join key is `CHAR(n)` wrap with `TRIM`/`RTRIM` | SME |
| SEM-04 | Timestamp / timezone | Possibly (`flgt_dt`, `gmt_flgt_dt`) | Not corrected — pending SME confirmation of types and session `TIMEZONE` pin | SME |
| SEM-05 | SET-table dedup | **Yes** | **Corrected** — single `INSERT … UNION … UNION` split into 3 independent `INSERT … (SELECT … EXCEPT SELECT * FROM staging.wrk_target)`; explicit 8-col INSERT list added | **Yes** (reviewer-confirmed SET table; EXCEPT preserves SET semantics) |
| SEM-06 | Implicit cast / NULL in comparison | Yes (`effective_dt \|\| val`, `flgt_dt > param_value`) | Not corrected — pending SME confirmation of `param_value` date format and `effective_dt` cast format | SME |
| SEM-07 | `NULL` ordering in `ORDER BY`/`QUALIFY` | No | n/a — no `ORDER BY`/`QUALIFY`/`TOP` | n/a |
| SEM-08 | Empty string vs NULL | No | n/a — no `''`/`NULLIF(x,'')` | n/a |
